 this in an automated  fashion and generate those kind of  reports  a second use case for this would be the  xbox and hololens team at microsoft  where they test computer vision on a  headless server so wolfram engine sits  on a server and it responds to various  calls as they test a variety of  proprietary computer vision algorithms  that are written in the wolfram language  and um i think this is a great use case  because one of the reasons why they use  wolfram is  these algorithms will eventually be  rewritten and likely compiled  for absolute speed when they get moved  into a product like the xbox  but the speed at which you can prototype  the new algorithms and test and refine  and test  in the wolfram language allows them to  refine and perfect those computer  vision algorithms much more quickly and  so one you know obvious use case for a  wolfram engine is  you know we want to use the high level  and and efficient nature of the wolfram  language to do a bunch of our testing  and algorithmic or modeling work until  we get it narrowed down to what we want  best  and then we'll move forward uh with that  and i think that's a great use for for  something like a wolfram engine  so the last technology we're going to  talk about is the wolf from enterprise  private cloud  i'm sure you guys many of you have heard  of the regular public cloud so i know of  wolf from cloud but how is the  enterprise private cloud different or  what is it  um so the epcs what we call the  enterprise private cloud is is the most  modern way for you to deploy  your web-based wolfram language content  in the form of applications or forms or  reports or tools or the fastest way of  deploying  just apis to your many services or  applications or programs  the epc is a self-contained appliance  consisting of a web application that's  installed on a virtual machine running  centos  it includes access to all wolfram cloud  web interfaces  with additional tools for for research  management user 